<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Large Language Models</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We perform an evaluation of the first-token log-probabilities of the following cloud-based models:</p>
<ul id="S5.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">OpenAI GPT-4o<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                    <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                    
                    
                    
                  Running on Microsoft Azure</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024</a>)</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Gemini 2.0 Flash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hassabis and Kavukcuoglu, 2024</a>)</cite></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Unlike open-source models, these models do not provide the full log-probabilities of generated tokens, but only the top 20.
We reconstructed the partial log-probabilities vectors, as the APIs of these models provide both the log-probability and token IDs (token indices)
of the top 20 tokens.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            For GPT-4o, we used <span id="footnote2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tiktoken</span> to retrieve the token IDs from the token string-values: <a href="https://github.com/openai/tiktoken" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_italic">github.com/openai/tiktoken</a></span></span></span>
Therefore, the vectors displayed in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.3 Large Language Models ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Do Stop Me Now: Detecting Boilerplate Responses with a Single Iteration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3 Large Language Models ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Do Stop Me Now: Detecting Boilerplate Responses with a Single Iteration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> are <span id="S5.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trimmed</span> log-probabilities vectors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Even with the trimmed log-probabilities, we still see clear separation between the classes.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.22679v1/images/llm_tsne.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/249;" width="476" height="249" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>2D T-SNE plot of first-token partial log-probabilities for Large Language Models (GPT-4o, Gemini-2.0 Flash).
Each point represents a chat, colored by class.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
